<p>Put a learner alone in a room with a problem slightly too hard for them and a microphone they have forgotten about, and the room stops being quiet. They read the question out loud. They read it again. They say the word they are looking for, get it wrong, say it again with the stress moved. They say <em>no, wait</em>, and then something in their first language. None of this is addressed to anybody. All of it is the sound of somebody trying to think.</p>

<p>Researchers call it private speech: speech directed at the self, for the self, usually at low volume, often inaudible unless you are standing close. It is the most common thing learners do and the least commonly trained. It is also, in the research literature, one of the better markers we have of whether a second language has stopped being a subject and started being a tool.</p>

<h2>What Private Speech Actually Is</h2>

<p>Vygotsky's account, from the 1930s, runs in three stages. A child first speaks socially, to other people. Then the same speech turns inward but stays audible, as private speech. Then it goes underground as inner speech - silent verbal thought. On this account, muttering to yourself is not a failed attempt at communication. It is the intermediate form, the scaffolding that gets dismantled once the structure stands.</p>

<p>The interesting part is what happens under load. Private speech does not disappear in adults; it re-emerges when a task exceeds what you can currently do silently. Ask an adult to assemble something awkward and they will start narrating. Ask a learner to explain a grammar point they half-know and they will start talking to the ceiling. The audible form comes back precisely when the internal one is not sufficient. That makes it a signal, not a tic: <strong>the moment you start muttering is the moment you have found your actual ceiling.</strong></p>

<h2>The Silent Period Was Never Silent</h2>

<p>The classic study here is Muriel Saville-Troike's 1988 paper in the <em>Journal of Child Language</em>, and its title gives the game away: <em>Private speech: evidence for second language learning strategies during the 'silent' period</em>.</p>

<p>Saville-Troike was interested in children who arrive in an English-medium school and say nothing for months. The standard reading was that nothing was happening - that they were waiting, or withdrawing. She fitted them with radio microphones and recorded them under natural conditions, including the times they were alone, and found that most of them were talking constantly. Just not to anyone.</p>

<p>The functions she catalogued included repetition of other people's utterances, recall and practice of language they had heard, syntagmatic expansion - building a longer string out of a shorter one they had picked up - and rehearsal for overt social performance, meaning they practised the sentence before they said it to a person. She also found the children using their first language to translate to themselves as they took in the new one. The quantity and quality of this speech tracked three things: the child's cognitive development, the difficulty of the task, and their social orientation and learning style.</p>

<p>She ranked the functions hierarchically. At the bottom sits self-stimulating language use - making sounds because sounds are satisfying. Above that, commentary and description. At the top, self-guidance: language used to direct your own next action.</p>

<blockquote>The silent period is not a period of silence. It is a period in which the learner's output has been redirected from the audience to the self.</blockquote>

<p>If you have ever spent three months in a language course saying almost nothing in class and then surprised yourself by producing a competent sentence, you already know this. The output was happening. It was just not addressed to the teacher.</p>

<h2>Ohta and the Vicarious Response</h2>

<p>Amy Snyder Ohta's 2001 study followed seven learners of Japanese through a full academic year, recording their classroom talk. What she found was that learners were answering questions that had been put to other people - quietly, under their breath, at their own desks - and repeating corrections that were aimed at somebody else's error.</p>

<p>She called it the vicarious response, and it is the single most under-counted form of practice in any classroom. A teacher who counts volunteered answers concludes that four students participated. A teacher who listens to the low muttering finds that most of the room was answering every question, including the ones it was not asked.</p>

<p>The self-study lesson is direct. You can be a vicarious responder to a podcast, an interview, a video. When the host asks the guest a question, pause and answer it before the guest does. When the speaker corrects themselves mid-sentence, say the corrected version under your breath. You are running the same mechanism on purpose, and you do not need a teacher to trigger it.</p>

<h2>Which Language Does the Muttering Switch Into?</h2>

<p>This is where the research gets genuinely useful, because the language of your private speech is a proficiency measure you can observe at home.</p>

<p>Centeno-Cort&eacute;s and Jim&eacute;nez Jim&eacute;nez published a study in the <em>International Journal of Applied Linguistics</em> in 2004 comparing three groups - intermediate learners of Spanish, advanced learners, and native speakers - as they worked through logical, mathematical and spatial problems in Spanish while being recorded alone. Intermediate learners did the reasoning in their first language. Advanced learners used Spanish while the problem was tractable and dropped back into the first language when it stopped being tractable. The authors concluded that the L1 was not a failure of discipline but a genuine tool: it surfaced as a key factor in the reasoning process.</p>

<p>Before that gets turned into a rule, two caveats matter. The first is that their "advanced" group had no more than about a year of informal exposure - a fact later critics used to argue the study was measuring proficiency, not a limit of the L2. The second is that the tasks were mathematical, and Spelke and Tsivkin showed in 2001 that bilinguals solve arithmetic problems most efficiently in the language they were trained in, whatever that language is. Numbers may be a special case.</p>

<p>A 2020 study in <em>Language and Sociocultural Theory</em> pushed on exactly that. Eleven adults - four native speakers of English and seven highly proficient non-native speakers with at least four years of formal study and four years of natural exposure - worked a three-stage logic puzzle aloud. The non-native speakers used no first language at all. Three of the seven solved all three levels and the other four completed two; none of the four native speakers got past the second level.</p>

<p>Read together, the two studies say something practical. <strong>The point at which you switch languages is a diagnostic, not a verdict.</strong> When you notice yourself thinking "wait, how do I say this" in your first language, you have located the exact operation that the second language cannot yet perform. That operation - usually numbers, kinship terms, spatial reasoning, or a causal chain - is your next set of cards.</p>

<h2>Your Inner Voice Follows Use, Not Study</h2>

<p>Jean-Marc Dewaele's work on multilingual inner speech, summarised in a 2018 review in <em>Language Teaching</em> titled <em>Going covert</em>, asked a simple question: in which language do multilinguals think?</p>

<p>The answers line up in an order worth memorising. Early acquirers - people who began the language between the ages of three and seven - used it for inner speech and mental arithmetic at higher rates than late acquirers. Age of onset predicted it. But the <strong>strongest predictor was frequency of use in everyday interaction</strong>, ahead of proficiency, socialisation, the size of your network of speakers, and where you learned it.</p>

<p>That is the least romantic finding in this article and the most useful. Inner speech is a usage statistic. A language you study for an hour a night but never use for anything does not become the medium of your thought, however good your recall is. A language you use badly for twenty minutes a day does start to colonise the inside of your head.</p>

<h2>Why Saying It Out Loud Works at All</h2>

<p>Gary Lupyan and Daniel Swingley gave a clean demonstration in the <em>Quarterly Journal of Experimental Psychology</em> in 2012. Participants searched a display for a target object. On some trials they said the target's name aloud first; on others they read it silently. Speaking the name made the search faster - but only for familiar, prototypical targets, ones with a strong association between the word and the visual thing. As the mismatch between the name and the target grew, speaking the name started to <em>impair</em> performance.</p>

<p>The mechanism they propose is label feedback: hearing the word activates the visual features that go with it, which makes the perceptual system temporarily a better detector of that category. The practical reading for a learner is narrow and precise. Saying a word aloud sharpens a category you already have. Saying a word aloud that you do not yet have a category for adds noise. So speak the labels you are consolidating, not the ones you are guessing at.</p>

<h2>The Words You Use to Yourself Matter</h2>

<p>One more result, from outside language learning, that applies directly to the moment before you open your mouth in a real conversation. Ethan Kross and colleagues, in the <em>Journal of Personality and Social Psychology</em> in 2014, ran seven studies with a total of 585 participants on how people refer to themselves during introspection.</p>

<p>Participants who used their own name or the pronoun "you" - rather than "I" - performed better on a public speaking task according to independent judges, reported less distress, and engaged in less post-event rumination. The effect was not moderated by trait social anxiety, which means it held for the people who needed it most. The proposed mechanism is self-distancing: referring to yourself by name creates enough psychological distance to appraise the situation as a challenge rather than a threat.</p>

<p>Translated into a second language: "You know this, Chris, just say the next part" is a better pre-speaking line than "I'm going to get this wrong". Do it in the target language and you are also rehearsing the register you need for self-encouragement, which is a real register that courses almost never teach.</p>

<h2>A Six-Step Protocol</h2>

<h3>1. Answer aloud before you flip</h3>

<p>Not after. The answer is produced out loud first, then the card is turned. You are converting a recognition test into a production test with an audible artefact, and the audible artefact makes it much harder to cheat yourself.</p>

<h3>2. Narrate the card, not just the word</h3>

<p>Ten seconds per card: <em>this means X, you would use it when Y, the one I keep confusing it with is Z.</em> That is private speech doing the job Saville-Troike catalogued - expansion and rehearsal - on material you have chosen rather than material you happened to hear.</p>

<h3>3. Write one line of the explanation in the target language</h3>

<p>Saville-Troike treated private writing as the close cousin of private speech: language recorded for yourself, to hold a form in place. One line per hard card is enough. It forces the grammar of explanation, which is different grammar from the grammar of example.</p>

<h3>4. Log your language of thought</h3>

<p>At the end of each session, sixty seconds: which cards did I think about in my first language? Those cards mark operations the second language cannot yet run. Sort them into a deck by domain - numbers, dates, family, spatial directions, cause and effect - and drill that deck until the switch stops happening.</p>

<h3>5. Rehearse the first sentence in the third person</h3>

<p>Before a call, a lesson, a shop. <em>You have done this a hundred times. Start with the greeting you know.</em> Kross's data says the naming does real work; your own data will tell you whether the target-language version feels natural or theatrical.</p>

<h3>6. Fade the volume deliberately</h3>

<p>Aloud, then whisper, then subvocal, then silent - and go back up a rung when the material gets harder. This is the Vygotskian ladder run in reverse, and it gives you a way to make the internalisation step explicit instead of hoping it happens.</p>

<h2>What the Evidence Cannot Tell You</h2>

<p>Honesty about the limits. Most of these studies are small: seven learners in Ohta's classroom, eleven adults in the 2020 puzzle study, a handful of children in Saville-Troike's recordings. Thinking aloud changes thinking - the reactivity problem - and private speech is genuinely hard to capture, since the interesting parts are the quietest. Several studies find no relationship between the <em>amount</em> of private speech and learning outcomes, and the researchers who work in this area increasingly argue that quality of function matters more than volume. There is also an extreme position, associated with Ushakova, that no language beyond the first can mediate thought at all, which the later proficiency studies contradict but have not demolished.</p>

<p>What survives the caveats is modest and actionable: learners talk to themselves while learning, the talking does identifiable work, the language it happens in tracks how much you actually use the language, and the moment it collapses back into your first language tells you exactly what to study next.</p>

<p>That is more than most study advice offers. It also costs nothing, needs no app, and can be started on the next card you turn over.</p>

